    {
      "name": "one byte over the published limit is silence, and spends nothing",
      "law": "The carriage",
      "obligations": ["III-6", "IX-5", "III-38"],
      "derivation": "The same ask with one more character in its one hint, which is one more byte in the envelope and nothing else. It is over the number this door published, so it is refused — and Article III since S-14: \"the `limit` binds on every road, distance zero included ... a door that accepted locally what it refuses over the common carriage would have made its own published number false.\" The refusal is before the judgment, so nothing is spent: the mark is still 1 and the way back still holds the hint the exchange above taught it. A door that judged this and then refused it would have moved the mark, which is what separates a carriage bound from a step of the judgment.",
